<Layout title="How Much Rent Can I Afford? | ShouldISpend">
  <main style="max-width: 760px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 72px 24px;">
    <p style="color:#0f766e; text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:2px; font-weight:800;">
      Housing Budget Guide
    </p>

    <h1 style="font-size: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5rem); line-height: .95;">
      How Much Rent Can I Afford?
    </h1>

    <p>
      Affordable rent depends on more than income alone. Savings, debt, emergency flexibility, transportation costs, and long-term goals all matter.
    </p>

    <h2>The 30% Rule</h2>

    <p>
      A common budgeting guideline recommends spending around 30% of gross monthly income on housing. In expensive cities, many households exceed that number.
    </p>

    <h2>When Rent Becomes Financially Dangerous</h2>

    <p>
      Rent may be too high when it consistently prevents saving money, paying down debt, building an emergency fund, or covering unexpected expenses comfortably.
    </p>

    <h2>What Should You Include in Housing Costs?</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>Monthly rent</li>
      <li>Utilities</li>
      <li>Parking</li>
      <li>Internet</li>
      <li>Renter’s insurance</li>
      <li>Transportation costs tied to location</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Related Calculators</h2>

    <ul>
      <li><a href="/should-i-spend-3000-on-rent">Should I Spend $3,000 on Rent?</a></li>
      <li><a href="/should-i-spend-50-percent-of-income-on-rent">Should I Spend 50% of My Income on Rent?</a></li>
    </ul>
  </main>
</Layout>